KG42.register("nodes-b8ed98b76c92c389b6bb0ecf",{"id":"COMP-79bf800032a75283","code":"CLR041","label":"Joint allocation minimizing aggregate member economic terms","definition":"Directly aggregate the economic contributions of collective members' asset operation and energy exchange, retain additional economic terms according to the source, and minimize this composite objective within the resource-coupled feasible region to select the joint energy configuration.","layer":"L5","template_code":"JOC","concept_id":"clearing.social_welfare","concept_ids":["clearing.local","clearing.social_welfare"],"identity_dimensions":{"definition":"Directly aggregate the economic contributions of collective members' asset operation and energy exchange, retain additional economic terms according to the source, and minimize this composite objective within the resource-coupled feasible region to select the joint energy configuration.","object":"Energy exchange and asset operation of collective members and their aggregate economic evaluation; household-level shares and public states of shared energy storage remain distinct.","inputs":"Member cost or utility functions, preferences and trading rules, equipment charging and compensation parameters, as well as the manager objective and resource feasibility conditions applicable to the source.","operation":"Sum member contributions and applicable additional terms according to each economic attribution and revenue/expenditure sign, and jointly minimize the objective; independent manager functions and member equipment revenues/expenditures are not forcibly mapped to the same term.","outputs":"The aggregate economic objective and corresponding member configurations; the public battery member additionally retains a shared state trajectory.","boundary":"Limited to L5/JOC; retain the member economic term aggregation identity of KG31-e0d9a80700a2295a, without presupposing a unified welfare net surplus meaning. Exclude taking welfare net surplus as a defining criterion, purchasing fixed demand solely according to supplier bids, minimizing the highest individual cost, and purely feasibility-based exchange. Charging, compensation direction, and price-sharing methods must not be unified."},"exclusions":[["Retain ψ_ij, h_j, and the independent manager function g; the common agreement can change through g and cannot be deleted into a mere electricity purchase expenditure.","Retain the roles and signs of net asset production, internal exchange, external imports, and exports; q_j is real while α_j and β_j are nonnegative.","Retain member balance, internal exchange balance, and the coupling of community total imports and total exports.","The global optimality guarantee is limited to cases that preserve overall convexity; nonconvex cases do not obtain this guarantee.","The centralized formulation requires disclosure of utility curves, and the equilibrium formulation assumes perfect competition; the KKT and efficiency statements are property descriptions and do not change the identity of the general aggregate objective here."],["Electricity purchase and discharge charges are positive expenditures, and charging compensation enters member net cost with a negative sign; compensation cannot be rewritten as physical depreciation cost.","Internal P2P revenues and expenditures do not affect the community objective value; public battery charges and compensation are still retained.","The shared energy storage state is updated according to all households' flows and charging/discharging efficiencies and is not split into independent private batteries.","Using grid electricity to charge the public battery is prohibited; capacity and rate conditions refer to equations (7) to (9), and parts not expanded are not supplemented.","The original objective uses p_D^{(t)}, while the price description uses household-specific p_D^{(t,h)} and associates it with their P2P prices; the two notations are stored separately, and no uniform discharge rate is presumed.","The textual description that charging is compensated at the wholesale spot price is retained; no price equation or numerical domain that is not provided is added."]],"members":[{"id":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::V::clearing.social_welfare","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"The community manager coordinates individually optimizing prosumers to achieve the collective optimum. Scope: Distributed optimization of the energy collective's aggregate cost/utility objective. Collective dispatch minimizes aggregate prosumer asset cost/utility and preference or transaction-cost contributions plus the community-manager objective. Scope: Collective dispatch objective in (1), with prosumer contributions expanded in (3). Under this convexity condition, the computed solution coincides with the global optimum; the source does not extend the global-optimality guarantee to non-convex formulations. Scope: Scope of the global-optimality guarantee for collective optimization. Condition: Asset cost/utility functions are convex and preserve convexity of the entire optimization problem. Every agent must reveal its utility curves in the centralized formulation. Scope: Centralized formulation of collective optimization. Condition: The market is solved as the central optimization problem. The equilibrium formulation assumes perfect competition. Scope: Equilibrium formulation used to establish market efficiency of collective optimization. Condition: The collective optimum is represented by the equilibrium problem.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Centralized formulation of collective optimization. | Collective dispatch objective in (1), with prosumer contributions expanded in (3). | Distributed optimization of the energy collective's aggregate cost/utility objective. | Equilibrium formulation used to establish market efficiency of collective optimization. | Scope of the global-optimality guarantee for collective optimization.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g01-E14","v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E17"]},"constraint_refs":["04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C004","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C005","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C006"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.social_welfare","source_rule_sha256":"b845186ce908b1fba76dace9ed1cd26a675acbfcd04d8499955fb9348255c747","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002","node_id":"clearing.social_welfare","function":"clearing.social_welfare","unit_id":"04-energy_collectives::v141-a-M01::clearing.social_welfare","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","market_meaning":"Charges for discharging the central battery and compensation for charging it enter the local-allocation objective as additional monetary components. Scope: Pool Hub local-allocation objective with centrally owned community storage. The allocation objective minimises grid-consumption costs plus central-battery discharge costs minus charging compensation. Internal P2P trade costs do not affect the community objective value. Scope: Pool Hub community allocation optimisation with centrally owned storage. Allocations obey the central battery's physical limits and a shared storage balance that aggregates charging and discharging flows across all houses. Scope: Pool Hub allocation feasibility with one shared central battery.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Pool Hub allocation feasibility with one shared central battery. | Pool Hub community allocation optimisation with centrally owned storage. | Pool Hub local-allocation objective with centrally owned community storage.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e14","v141-b-g02-e15","v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g03-E11"]},"constraint_refs":["26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C001","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C002","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C003","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C004"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"6f03d5dc7c2a468808ca21954b5e7e46d7d4fa74e880a279cf66d8f68dff37b4","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008","node_id":"clearing.local","function":"clearing.local","unit_id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-a-M02::clearing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M02","anchor":true}],"paper_ids":["04-energy_collectives","26-battery_flexibility"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p02-c1r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":"3995","section":"I. Introduction","anchor_text":"exist to enable these market organizations, for instance on the cloud for distributed systems [9] or","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We call this market organization energy collective and we define it as a community of prosumers that operates in a collaborative manner, optimizing usage of resources. This translates to a market framework where the collective members can trade their lack or excess of energy. The distributed nature of the market implies that all prosumers are in charge of optimizing their assets individually. Optimality is achieved as prosumers are coordinated by a non-profit virtual node, that we call community manager.","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"We call this market organization energy collective and we define it as a community of prosumers that operates in a collaborative manner, optimizing usage of resources. This translates to a market framework where the collective members can trade their lack or excess of energy. The distributed nature of the market implies that all prosumers are in charge of optimizing their assets individually. Optimality is achieved as prosumers are coordinated by a non-profit virtual node, that we call community manager."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"as the solution of the exchange problem ad n of min fj(pj, qj, αj, βj) + g(qimp, qexp, ϑ) (1a) ap Γ ","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The optimal community dispatch is then obtained as the solution of the exchange problem: min_{Gamma} sum_{j=1}^{n} f_j(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) + g(q_imp, q_exp, theta)   (1a)  s.t. p_j + q_j + alpha_j - beta_j = 0 , j = 1, . . . , n   (1b)  sum_{j=1}^{n} q_j = 0   (1c)  sum_{j=1}^{n} alpha_j = q_imp   (1d)  sum_{j=1}^{n} beta_j = q_exp   (1e)  p_j in P_j , j = 1, . . . , n   (1f)  alpha_j, beta_j >= 0 , j = 1, . . . , n   (1g)  where Gamma = {p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j} is the set of decision variables and q_imp, q_exp are continuous variables describing import and export exchanges with the system operator.","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"The optimal community dispatch is then obtained as the solution of the exchange problem: min_{Gamma} sum_{j=1}^{n} f_j(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) + g(q_imp, q_exp, theta)   (1a)  s.t. p_j + q_j + alpha_j - beta_j = 0 , j = 1, . . . , n   (1b)  sum_{j=1}^{n} q_j = 0   (1c)  sum_{j=1}^{n} alpha_j = q_imp   (1d)  sum_{j=1}^{n} beta_j = q_exp   (1e)  p_j in P_j , j = 1, . . . , n   (1f)  alpha_j, beta_j >= 0 , j = 1, . . . , n   (1g)  where Gamma = {p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j} is the set of decision variables and q_imp, q_exp are continuous variables describing import and export exchanges with the system operator."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c2r1__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 ers the technology constraints, U","equation_id":"(2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For the sake of this work, we assume quadratic costs and utility functions for each asset, psi_i,j(u_ij) = c_ij u_ij^2 + d_ij u_ij   (2)  with c_ij >= 0. However, all the considerations in this paper hold as long as the functions psi_ij are convex, preserving the convexity of the entire optimization problem and, hence, ensuring that the solution found coincides with the global optimum.","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"For the sake of this work, we assume quadratic costs and utility functions for each asset, psi_i,j(u_ij) = c_ij u_ij^2 + d_ij u_ij   (2)  with c_ij >= 0. However, all the considerations in this paper hold as long as the functions psi_ij are convex, preserving the convexity of the entire optimization problem and, hence, ensuring that the solution found coincides with the global optimum."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c2r2__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"adjusted to reach optimality. However, as there is no guarantee of global but only local optimality,","equation_id":"(3)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Since we aim for an assets independent formulation, prosumers are defined in the community market only by their net production p_j = sum_{i in I_j} u_ij. In order to decompose the global optimization problem into sub-problems specific for each prosumer j, we define f-tilde_j as an extension of f in (1), including all prosumer-specific information, as  f-tilde_j(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) = sum_{i in I_j} psi_ij(u_ij) + h_j(q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) + I_{R_j}(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j)   (3)  We use the indicator function I_{R_j}(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) on the set  R_j = { (p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) | p_j = sum_{i in I_j} u_ij, u_ij in U_ij for i in I_j, p_j + q_j + alpha_j - beta_j = 0, alpha_j >= 0, beta_j >= 0 }","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Since we aim for an assets independent formulation, prosumers are defined in the community market only by their net production p_j = sum_{i in I_j} u_ij. In order to decompose the global optimization problem into sub-problems specific for each prosumer j, we define f-tilde_j as an extension of f in (1), including all prosumer-specific information, as  f-tilde_j(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) = sum_{i in I_j} psi_ij(u_ij) + h_j(q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) + I_{R_j}(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j)   (3)  We use the indicator function I_{R_j}(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) on the set  R_j = { (p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) | p_j = sum_{i in I_j} u_ij, u_ij in U_ij for i in I_j, p_j + q_j + alpha_j - beta_j = 0, alpha_j >= 0, beta_j >= 0 }"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"i∈Ij 1g) and pj + qj + αj −βj = 0, αj ≥0, βj ≥0 ort on- to include all the constraints related to th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"to include all the constraints related to the single prosumers (P_j [relation symbol not clearly resolved from print] intersection_{i in I_j} U_i,j) and the function h to include homogeneous costs previously agreed within the community, e.g. transaction costs, as well as heterogeneous preference schemes. For instance, if we assume h to be  h_j(q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) = gamma_com |q_j| + gamma_imp alpha_j + gamma_exp beta_j  the optimization of prosumer j, and hence the equilibrium of the whole collective, is influenced accordingly to the weighting coefficients. Favourable transaction costs for internal energy exchanges, gamma_com, will encourage prosumer j to trade more inside the community, while extra penalization for import and export of energy, (gamma_imp, gamma_exp), may reflect the will of prosumer j to increase its own autonomy from market and system operator.","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"to include all the constraints related to the single prosumers (P_j [relation symbol not clearly resolved from print] intersection_{i in I_j} U_i,j) and the function h to include homogeneous costs previously agreed within the community, e.g. transaction costs, as well as heterogeneous preference schemes. For instance, if we assume h to be  h_j(q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) = gamma_com |q_j| + gamma_imp alpha_j + gamma_exp beta_j  the optimization of prosumer j, and hence the equilibrium of the whole collective, is influenced accordingly to the weighting coefficients. Favourable transaction costs for internal energy exchanges, gamma_com, will encourage prosumer j to trade more inside the community, while extra penalization for import and export of energy, (gamma_imp, gamma_exp), may reflect the will of prosumer j to increase its own autonomy from market and system operator."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"αj, βj ≥0 , j = 1, . . . , n (1g) where Γ = {pj, qj, αj, βj} is the set of decision variables and qi","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The core of this market organization is the inclusion, in the negotiation process, of common agreements on how the energy collective is to jointly handle its internal objectives and its interaction with the outside world. We address this role to the community manager and we model it in (1) by means of the function g. We define g only by global variables (q_imp and q_exp) and by a set of relevant parameters or additionally variables theta. Different community agreements can be included only by operating on the community manager, without modifying the prosumers model.","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"The core of this market organization is the inclusion, in the negotiation process, of common agreements on how the energy collective is to jointly handle its internal objectives and its interaction with the outside world. We address this role to the community manager and we model it in (1) by means of the function g. We define g only by global variables (q_imp and q_exp) and by a set of relevant parameters or additionally variables theta. Different community agreements can be included only by operating on the community manager, without modifying the prosumers model."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E32","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"IV. Distributed Optimization Approach to the Operation of Energy Collectives","anchor_text":"In order to decompose the community economic dispatch ﬁg- with ADMM, we rewrite (1) in terms of (3).","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In order to decompose the community economic dispatch with ADMM, we rewrite (1) in terms of (3). It follows that  min_{Gamma} sum_{j=1}^{n} f-tilde_j(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) + g(q_imp, q_exp)   (13a)  s.t. sum_{j=1}^{n} q_j = 0   (13b)  sum_{j=1}^{n} alpha_j = q_imp   (13c)  sum_{j=1}^{n} beta_j = q_exp   (13d)","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"In order to decompose the community economic dispatch with ADMM, we rewrite (1) in terms of (3). It follows that  min_{Gamma} sum_{j=1}^{n} f-tilde_j(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) + g(q_imp, q_exp)   (13a)  s.t. sum_{j=1}^{n} q_j = 0   (13b)  sum_{j=1}^{n} alpha_j = q_imp   (13c)  sum_{j=1}^{n} beta_j = q_exp   (13d)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E14","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c2r1__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 ers the technology constraints, U","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Note that in case of non-convex problems decomposition techniques can be adjusted to reach optimality. However, as there is no guarantee of global but only local optimality, it is common procedure to apply out-of-market corrections to cope with suboptimality.","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Note that in case of non-convex problems decomposition techniques can be adjusted to reach optimality. However, as there is no guarantee of global but only local optimality, it is common procedure to apply out-of-market corrections to cope with suboptimality."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e19","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-validation","page":6,"printed_page":"3999","section":"V. Application and Case-Studies","anchor_text":"ADMM provides a general method that grants convergence independently from the nature of the optimiza","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"ADMM provides a general method that grants convergence independently from the nature of the optimization problem. As we find ADMM to be sensitive to numerical properties of the dataset, application-specific methods may achieve better performances. However, we continue with ADMM, since relatively easy heuristic schemes solve the convergence issues for most of the simulations of this paper. Furthermore, the focus of this work relies more on the market structure and operational framework of energy collectives rather than on its solving methodology.","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"ADMM provides a general method that grants convergence independently from the nature of the optimization problem. As we find ADMM to be sensitive to numerical properties of the dataset, application-specific methods may achieve better performances. However, we continue with ADMM, since relatively easy heuristic schemes solve the convergence issues for most of the simulations of this paper. Furthermore, the focus of this work relies more on the market structure and operational framework of energy collectives rather than on its solving methodology."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"xp) increase its own autonomy from market and system operator. les by C. Market Efﬁciency and Incent","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The proposed market design for energy collectives ensures, by construction, market efficiency. As demonstrated in the Appendix, the Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) conditions of the optimization problem, as in (1), correspond to those of an equilibrium problem in which each agent individually minimizes its costs. This implies that both problems achieve the same optimal solution under different assumptions. Perfect competition is assumed in the equilibrium problem solution, while in the central optimization problem every agent has to reveal its utility curves. However, by decomposing the optimization problem, we are able to limit the set of information needed to grant market efficiency, enhancing the privacy level of the participants utility and cost curves as well as their preferences.","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"The proposed market design for energy collectives ensures, by construction, market efficiency. As demonstrated in the Appendix, the Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) conditions of the optimization problem, as in (1), correspond to those of an equilibrium problem in which each agent individually minimizes its costs. This implies that both problems achieve the same optimal solution under different assumptions. Perfect competition is assumed in the equilibrium problem solution, while in the central optimization problem every agent has to reveal its utility curves. However, by decomposing the optimization problem, we are able to limit the set of information needed to grant market efficiency, enhancing the privacy level of the participants utility and cost curves as well as their preferences."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p11-c1r1__v141-a-M01-validation","page":11,"printed_page":"4004","section":"Appendix","anchor_text":"4004 IE As the two sets of KKT conditions, (17) and (21), are equal, [1 this demonstrates that the o","equation_id":"(16), (17), (18), (21)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-11.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"As the two sets of KKT conditions, (17) and (21), are equal, this demonstrates that the optimization problem (16) for the market-based operation of energy collectives and the equilibrium problem (18)–(20) solve the same problem. This translates to ensuring market efficiency.","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"As the two sets of KKT conditions, (17) and (21), are equal, this demonstrates that the optimization problem (16) for the market-based operation of energy collectives and the equilibrium problem (18)–(20) solve the same problem. This translates to ensuring market efficiency."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e36","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p09-c2r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":9,"printed_page":"4002","section":"Appendix","anchor_text":"to the clearing algorithms, especially to their performances in the more realistic case of asynchron","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"APPENDIX\n\nIn this appendix we demonstrate market efficiency for the proposed energy collectives. Our approach is to show that the energy collective market, modelled as a centralized optimization problem, solves exactly the same problem as an equilibrium problem in which every agent optimizes its energy procurement while subjected to market constraints. In practice, this is done by checking that the KKT conditions of both problems coincide. We start by considering the extended version of (1) as","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"APPENDIX\n\nIn this appendix we demonstrate market efficiency for the proposed energy collectives. Our approach is to show that the energy collective market, modelled as a centralized optimization problem, solves exactly the same problem as an equilibrium problem in which every agent optimizes its energy procurement while subjected to market constraints. In practice, this is done by checking that the KKT conditions of both problems coincide. We start by considering the extended version of (1) as"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p06-c1r1__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.2 Market specific constraints","anchor_text":"A. Lüth et al. Grid consumption Battery discharge Battery charge re ⎛    ⎞ min ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In the Pool Hub case with a centralised storage, costs arise for three components: grid consumption, P2P trade and discharging the centralised battery. Furthermore, we compensate prosumers for charging and considers their incomes from P2P trade, see Fig. 2(b). The objective function of the Pool Hub, Eq. (11), needs hence two more components that add costs for battery discharging D^{(t,h)} and compensations for charging C^{(t,h)}. \\min \\sum_h \\left( \\sum_t [p_G^{(t)} \\cdot G^{(t,h)}] + \\sum_t [p_D^{(t)} \\cdot D^{(t,h)}] - \\sum_t [p_C^{(t)} \\cdot C^{(t,h)}] \\right) (11).","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","quote":"In the Pool Hub case with a centralised storage, costs arise for three components: grid consumption, P2P trade and discharging the centralised battery. Furthermore, we compensate prosumers for charging and considers their incomes from P2P trade, see Fig. 2(b). The objective function of the Pool Hub, Eq. (11), needs hence two more components that add costs for battery discharging D^{(t,h)} and compensations for charging C^{(t,h)}. \\min \\sum_h \\left( \\sum_t [p_G^{(t)} \\cdot G^{(t,h)}] + \\sum_t [p_D^{(t)} \\cdot D^{(t,h)}] - \\sum_t [p_C^{(t)} \\cdot C^{(t,h)}] \\right) (11)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.3 Pool Hub market results","anchor_text":"ho Half of the demand can be covered from distributed generation • an within the community. sy ra 4.","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"The Pool Hub market design considers an interconnected community of consumers with a central storage device. P2P trade is priced at the same dynamic rate as in the Flexi User case. As the storage is no longer owned privately, discharging from the central battery will be priced and charging compensated. Charging the battery from the grid is not allowed.","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","quote":"The Pool Hub market design considers an interconnected community of consumers with a central storage device. P2P trade is priced at the same dynamic rate as in the Flexi User case. As the storage is no longer owned privately, discharging from the central battery will be priced and charging compensated. Charging the battery from the grid is not allowed."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p07-c1r4__v141-a-M02-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1.4 Electricity prices","anchor_text":"step. Since this P2P price should not consider the fees for network and sp grid usage (typically one","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For the Pool Hub market, we need two more dynamic price schemes. As the charging of the central battery should be compensated, prosumers receive the wholesale spot price for electricity when they charge the battery. The discharging is priced according to each house’s willingness to pay (i.e. the P2P price p_{p2p}^{(t,h)}) plus an additional fee to avoid arbitrage operation through the battery. This additional fee is set equal to the charging compensation p_C^{(t)} at this time. Thus, the price for discharging is equal to the sum of the P2P trade price p_{p2p}^{(t,h)} and the charging compensation p_C^{(t)} and differs among the houses.","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","quote":"For the Pool Hub market, we need two more dynamic price schemes. As the charging of the central battery should be compensated, prosumers receive the wholesale spot price for electricity when they charge the battery. The discharging is priced according to each house’s willingness to pay (i.e. the P2P price p_{p2p}^{(t,h)}) plus an additional fee to avoid arbitrage operation through the battery. This additional fee is set equal to the charging compensation p_C^{(t)} at this time. Thus, the price for discharging is equal to the sum of the P2P trade price p_{p2p}^{(t,h)} and the charging compensation p_C^{(t)} and differs among the houses."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p09-c1r3__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.4 Comparison","anchor_text":"Fig. 4. Demand cover for exemplary houses on changed the generation and demand proﬁles of the houses","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"As trade costs do not affect the objective value of the community, the match of aggregated supply and demand influences the results.","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","quote":"As trade costs do not affect the objective value of the community, the match of aggregated supply and demand influences the results."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e15","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p06-c1r1__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.2 Market specific constraints","anchor_text":"A. Lüth et al. Grid consumption Battery discharge Battery charge re ⎛    ⎞ min ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The big battery is limited to the physical constraints formulated in Eqs. (7)–(9) with the constants \\underline{s}, \\bar{s}, \\alpha, \\beta, \\eta^c and \\eta^d being different for the greater centralised storage entity. The overall storage level will no longer be depending on a single house’s charge or discharge, but takes into account the sum of all flows from and to all houses to the battery. Eq. (12) comprises the sums of discharge D^{(t,h)} and charge C^{(t,h)} for the centralised battery and adds these up to the overall storage level S^{(t)}. S^{(t)} = S^{(t-1)} + \\eta^c \\cdot \\sum_h C^{(t,h)} - (1/\\eta^d) \\cdot \\sum_h D^{(t,h)}, \\quad \\forall t \\in T. (12).","paper":"26-battery_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","quote":"The big battery is limited to the physical constraints formulated in Eqs. (7)–(9) with the constants \\underline{s}, \\bar{s}, \\alpha, \\beta, \\eta^c and \\eta^d being different for the greater centralised storage entity. The overall storage level will no longer be depending on a single house’s charge or discharge, but takes into account the sum of all flows from and to all houses to the battery. Eq. (12) comprises the sums of discharge D^{(t,h)} and charge C^{(t,h)} for the centralised battery and adds these up to the overall storage level S^{(t)}. S^{(t)} = S^{(t-1)} + \\eta^c \\cdot \\sum_h C^{(t,h)} - (1/\\eta^d) \\cdot \\sum_h D^{(t,h)}, \\quad \\forall t \\in T. (12)."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["aggregate_cost_minimization","community_net_energy_cost_minimizing_allocation"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"kind-L5-979b7eef7d","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L5","ontological_kind":"aggregate_cost_minimization"},"semantic_definition":{"definition":"Directly aggregate the economic contributions of collective members' asset operation and energy exchange, retain additional economic terms according to the source, and minimize this composite objective within the resource-coupled feasible region to select the joint energy configuration.","object":"Energy exchange and asset operation of collective members and their aggregate economic evaluation; household-level shares and public states of shared energy storage remain distinct.","inputs":"Member cost or utility functions, preferences and trading rules, equipment charging and compensation parameters, as well as the manager objective and resource feasibility conditions applicable to the source.","operation":"Sum member contributions and applicable additional terms according to each economic attribution and revenue/expenditure sign, and jointly minimize the objective; independent manager functions and member equipment revenues/expenditures are not forcibly mapped to the same term.","outputs":"The aggregate economic objective and corresponding member configurations; the public battery member additionally retains a shared state trajectory.","boundary":"Limited to L5/JOC; retain the member economic term aggregation identity of KG31-e0d9a80700a2295a, without presupposing a unified welfare net surplus meaning. Exclude taking welfare net surplus as a defining criterion, purchasing fixed demand solely according to supplier bids, minimizing the highest individual cost, and purely feasibility-based exchange. Charging, compensation direction, and price-sharing methods must not be unified."},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"04-energy_collectives::v141-a-M01::clearing.social_welfare","paper_id":"04-energy_collectives","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"clearing.social_welfare","source_occurrences":[{"id":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::V::clearing.social_welfare","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"The community manager coordinates individually optimizing prosumers to achieve the collective optimum. Scope: Distributed optimization of the energy collective's aggregate cost/utility objective. Collective dispatch minimizes aggregate prosumer asset cost/utility and preference or transaction-cost contributions plus the community-manager objective. Scope: Collective dispatch objective in (1), with prosumer contributions expanded in (3). Under this convexity condition, the computed solution coincides with the global optimum; the source does not extend the global-optimality guarantee to non-convex formulations. Scope: Scope of the global-optimality guarantee for collective optimization. Condition: Asset cost/utility functions are convex and preserve convexity of the entire optimization problem. Every agent must reveal its utility curves in the centralized formulation. Scope: Centralized formulation of collective optimization. Condition: The market is solved as the central optimization problem. The equilibrium formulation assumes perfect competition. Scope: Equilibrium formulation used to establish market efficiency of collective optimization. Condition: The collective optimum is represented by the equilibrium problem.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Centralized formulation of collective optimization. | Collective dispatch objective in (1), with prosumer contributions expanded in (3). | Distributed optimization of the energy collective's aggregate cost/utility objective. | Equilibrium formulation used to establish market efficiency of collective optimization. | Scope of the global-optimality guarantee for collective optimization.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g01-E14","v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E17"]},"constraint_refs":["04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C004","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C005","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C006"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.social_welfare","source_rule_sha256":"b845186ce908b1fba76dace9ed1cd26a675acbfcd04d8499955fb9348255c747"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The community manager coordinates individually optimizing prosumers to achieve the collective optimum. Scope: Distributed optimization of the energy collective's aggregate cost/utility objective.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E09"],"ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p02-c1r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":"3995","section":"I. Introduction","anchor_text":"exist to enable these market organizations, for instance on the cloud for distributed systems [9] or","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We call this market organization energy collective and we define it as a community of prosumers that operates in a collaborative manner, optimizing usage of resources. This translates to a market framework where the collective members can trade their lack or excess of energy. The distributed nature of the market implies that all prosumers are in charge of optimizing their assets individually. Optimality is achieved as prosumers are coordinated by a non-profit virtual node, that we call community manager."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"as the solution of the exchange problem ad n of min fj(pj, qj, αj, βj) + g(qimp, qexp, ϑ) (1a) ap Γ ","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The optimal community dispatch is then obtained as the solution of the exchange problem: min_{Gamma} sum_{j=1}^{n} f_j(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) + g(q_imp, q_exp, theta)   (1a)  s.t. p_j + q_j + alpha_j - beta_j = 0 , j = 1, . . . , n   (1b)  sum_{j=1}^{n} q_j = 0   (1c)  sum_{j=1}^{n} alpha_j = q_imp   (1d)  sum_{j=1}^{n} beta_j = q_exp   (1e)  p_j in P_j , j = 1, . . . , n   (1f)  alpha_j, beta_j >= 0 , j = 1, . . . , n   (1g)  where Gamma = {p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j} is the set of decision variables and q_imp, q_exp are continuous variables describing import and export exchanges with the system operator."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Collective dispatch minimizes aggregate prosumer asset cost/utility and preference or transaction-cost contributions plus the community-manager objective. Scope: Collective dispatch objective in (1), with prosumer contributions expanded in (3).","expression":"min_{Gamma} sum_{j=1}^{n} f-tilde_j(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) + g(q_imp, q_exp, theta)   (1a expanded via (3)); f-tilde_j(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) = sum_{i in I_j} psi_ij(u_ij) + h_j(q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) + I_{R_j}(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j)   (3)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":["p_j","q_j","alpha_j","beta_j","q_imp","q_exp","u_ij"],"parameter_refs":["theta","n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E11","v141-b-g01-E32"],"ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"as the solution of the exchange problem ad n of min fj(pj, qj, αj, βj) + g(qimp, qexp, ϑ) (1a) ap Γ ","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The optimal community dispatch is then obtained as the solution of the exchange problem: min_{Gamma} sum_{j=1}^{n} f_j(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) + g(q_imp, q_exp, theta)   (1a)  s.t. p_j + q_j + alpha_j - beta_j = 0 , j = 1, . . . , n   (1b)  sum_{j=1}^{n} q_j = 0   (1c)  sum_{j=1}^{n} alpha_j = q_imp   (1d)  sum_{j=1}^{n} beta_j = q_exp   (1e)  p_j in P_j , j = 1, . . . , n   (1f)  alpha_j, beta_j >= 0 , j = 1, . . . , n   (1g)  where Gamma = {p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j} is the set of decision variables and q_imp, q_exp are continuous variables describing import and export exchanges with the system operator."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c2r1__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 ers the technology constraints, U","equation_id":"(2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For the sake of this work, we assume quadratic costs and utility functions for each asset, psi_i,j(u_ij) = c_ij u_ij^2 + d_ij u_ij   (2)  with c_ij >= 0. However, all the considerations in this paper hold as long as the functions psi_ij are convex, preserving the convexity of the entire optimization problem and, hence, ensuring that the solution found coincides with the global optimum."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c2r2__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"adjusted to reach optimality. However, as there is no guarantee of global but only local optimality,","equation_id":"(3)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Since we aim for an assets independent formulation, prosumers are defined in the community market only by their net production p_j = sum_{i in I_j} u_ij. In order to decompose the global optimization problem into sub-problems specific for each prosumer j, we define f-tilde_j as an extension of f in (1), including all prosumer-specific information, as  f-tilde_j(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) = sum_{i in I_j} psi_ij(u_ij) + h_j(q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) + I_{R_j}(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j)   (3)  We use the indicator function I_{R_j}(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) on the set  R_j = { (p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) | p_j = sum_{i in I_j} u_ij, u_ij in U_ij for i in I_j, p_j + q_j + alpha_j - beta_j = 0, alpha_j >= 0, beta_j >= 0 }"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"i∈Ij 1g) and pj + qj + αj −βj = 0, αj ≥0, βj ≥0 ort on- to include all the constraints related to th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"to include all the constraints related to the single prosumers (P_j [relation symbol not clearly resolved from print] intersection_{i in I_j} U_i,j) and the function h to include homogeneous costs previously agreed within the community, e.g. transaction costs, as well as heterogeneous preference schemes. For instance, if we assume h to be  h_j(q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) = gamma_com |q_j| + gamma_imp alpha_j + gamma_exp beta_j  the optimization of prosumer j, and hence the equilibrium of the whole collective, is influenced accordingly to the weighting coefficients. Favourable transaction costs for internal energy exchanges, gamma_com, will encourage prosumer j to trade more inside the community, while extra penalization for import and export of energy, (gamma_imp, gamma_exp), may reflect the will of prosumer j to increase its own autonomy from market and system operator."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"αj, βj ≥0 , j = 1, . . . , n (1g) where Γ = {pj, qj, αj, βj} is the set of decision variables and qi","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The core of this market organization is the inclusion, in the negotiation process, of common agreements on how the energy collective is to jointly handle its internal objectives and its interaction with the outside world. We address this role to the community manager and we model it in (1) by means of the function g. We define g only by global variables (q_imp and q_exp) and by a set of relevant parameters or additionally variables theta. Different community agreements can be included only by operating on the community manager, without modifying the prosumers model."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E32","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"IV. Distributed Optimization Approach to the Operation of Energy Collectives","anchor_text":"In order to decompose the community economic dispatch ﬁg- with ADMM, we rewrite (1) in terms of (3).","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In order to decompose the community economic dispatch with ADMM, we rewrite (1) in terms of (3). It follows that  min_{Gamma} sum_{j=1}^{n} f-tilde_j(p_j, q_j, alpha_j, beta_j) + g(q_imp, q_exp)   (13a)  s.t. sum_{j=1}^{n} q_j = 0   (13b)  sum_{j=1}^{n} alpha_j = q_imp   (13c)  sum_{j=1}^{n} beta_j = q_exp   (13d)"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C003","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"Under this convexity condition, the computed solution coincides with the global optimum; the source does not extend the global-optimality guarantee to non-convex formulations. Scope: Scope of the global-optimality guarantee for collective optimization. Condition: Asset cost/utility functions are convex and preserve convexity of the entire optimization problem.","expression":"convex(psi_ij) for all i,j ⇒ the entire optimization problem is convex and the solution found coincides with the global optimum","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g01-E14","v141-b-g02-e19"],"ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c2r1__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 ers the technology constraints, U","equation_id":"(2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For the sake of this work, we assume quadratic costs and utility functions for each asset, psi_i,j(u_ij) = c_ij u_ij^2 + d_ij u_ij   (2)  with c_ij >= 0. However, all the considerations in this paper hold as long as the functions psi_ij are convex, preserving the convexity of the entire optimization problem and, hence, ensuring that the solution found coincides with the global optimum."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E14","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c2r1__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 ers the technology constraints, U","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Note that in case of non-convex problems decomposition techniques can be adjusted to reach optimality. However, as there is no guarantee of global but only local optimality, it is common procedure to apply out-of-market corrections to cope with suboptimality."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e19","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-validation","page":6,"printed_page":"3999","section":"V. Application and Case-Studies","anchor_text":"ADMM provides a general method that grants convergence independently from the nature of the optimiza","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"ADMM provides a general method that grants convergence independently from the nature of the optimization problem. As we find ADMM to be sensitive to numerical properties of the dataset, application-specific methods may achieve better performances. However, we continue with ADMM, since relatively easy heuristic schemes solve the convergence issues for most of the simulations of this paper. Furthermore, the focus of this work relies more on the market structure and operational framework of energy collectives rather than on its solving methodology."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Every agent must reveal its utility curves in the centralized formulation. Scope: Centralized formulation of collective optimization. Condition: The market is solved as the central optimization problem.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E17"],"ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"xp) increase its own autonomy from market and system operator. les by C. Market Efﬁciency and Incent","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The proposed market design for energy collectives ensures, by construction, market efficiency. As demonstrated in the Appendix, the Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) conditions of the optimization problem, as in (1), correspond to those of an equilibrium problem in which each agent individually minimizes its costs. This implies that both problems achieve the same optimal solution under different assumptions. Perfect competition is assumed in the equilibrium problem solution, while in the central optimization problem every agent has to reveal its utility curves. However, by decomposing the optimization problem, we are able to limit the set of information needed to grant market efficiency, enhancing the privacy level of the participants utility and cost curves as well as their preferences."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The equilibrium formulation assumes perfect competition. Scope: Equilibrium formulation used to establish market efficiency of collective optimization. Condition: The collective optimum is represented by the equilibrium problem.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E17"],"ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"xp) increase its own autonomy from market and system operator. les by C. Market Efﬁciency and Incent","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The proposed market design for energy collectives ensures, by construction, market efficiency. As demonstrated in the Appendix, the Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) conditions of the optimization problem, as in (1), correspond to those of an equilibrium problem in which each agent individually minimizes its costs. This implies that both problems achieve the same optimal solution under different assumptions. Perfect competition is assumed in the equilibrium problem solution, while in the central optimization problem every agent has to reveal its utility curves. However, by decomposing the optimization problem, we are able to limit the set of information needed to grant market efficiency, enhancing the privacy level of the participants utility and cost curves as well as their preferences."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C006","kind":"logical","role":"supporting","statement":"The KKT conditions of the centralized social-welfare optimization problem and of the equilibrium problem coincide, so both problems achieve the same optimal solution.","expression":"KKT conditions of (16) coincide with those of (18)-(20) ⇒ optimization problem (16) and equilibrium problem (18)-(20) solve the same problem","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.social_welfare","coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E17","v141-b-g03-E20","v141-b-g02-e36"],"ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C006","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":3,"printed_page":"3996","section":"II. Energy Collectives and Their Operational Framework","anchor_text":"xp) increase its own autonomy from market and system operator. les by C. Market Efﬁciency and Incent","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The proposed market design for energy collectives ensures, by construction, market efficiency. As demonstrated in the Appendix, the Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) conditions of the optimization problem, as in (1), correspond to those of an equilibrium problem in which each agent individually minimizes its costs. This implies that both problems achieve the same optimal solution under different assumptions. Perfect competition is assumed in the equilibrium problem solution, while in the central optimization problem every agent has to reveal its utility curves. However, by decomposing the optimization problem, we are able to limit the set of information needed to grant market efficiency, enhancing the privacy level of the participants utility and cost curves as well as their preferences."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p11-c1r1__v141-a-M01-validation","page":11,"printed_page":"4004","section":"Appendix","anchor_text":"4004 IE As the two sets of KKT conditions, (17) and (21), are equal, [1 this demonstrates that the o","equation_id":"(16), (17), (18), (21)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-11.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"As the two sets of KKT conditions, (17) and (21), are equal, this demonstrates that the optimization problem (16) for the market-based operation of energy collectives and the equilibrium problem (18)–(20) solve the same problem. This translates to ensuring market efficiency."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e36","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p09-c2r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":9,"printed_page":"4002","section":"Appendix","anchor_text":"to the clearing algorithms, especially to their performances in the more realistic case of asynchron","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"APPENDIX\n\nIn this appendix we demonstrate market efficiency for the proposed energy collectives. Our approach is to show that the energy collective market, modelled as a centralized optimization problem, solves exactly the same problem as an equilibrium problem in which every agent optimizes its energy procurement while subjected to market constraints. In practice, this is done by checking that the KKT conditions of both problems coincide. We start by considering the extended version of (1) as"}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"p_j","symbol":"p_j","meaning":"Net energy produced by prosumer j","kind":"operational","domain":"P_j (feasible space of p_j; p_j ∈ P_j in (1f))","unit":null,"indices":["j = 1, ..., n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E02","v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::x::p_j"},{"variable_id":"q_j","symbol":"q_j","meaning":"Energy imported by prosumer j from the community","kind":"operational","domain":"real (continuous); enters community balance sum_{j=1}^n q_j = 0","unit":null,"indices":["j = 1, ..., n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E02","v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::x::q_j"},{"variable_id":"alpha_j","symbol":"alpha_j","meaning":"Energy imported by prosumer j","kind":"operational","domain":"nonnegative real (alpha_j >= 0 in (1g))","unit":null,"indices":["j = 1, ..., n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E02","v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::x::alpha_j"},{"variable_id":"beta_j","symbol":"beta_j","meaning":"Energy exported by prosumer j","kind":"operational","domain":"nonnegative real (beta_j >= 0 in (1g))","unit":null,"indices":["j = 1, ..., n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E02","v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::x::beta_j"},{"variable_id":"q_imp","symbol":"q_imp","meaning":"Total energy imported by the community from the system operator","kind":"operational","domain":"continuous; q_imp = sum_{j=1}^n alpha_j (1d), hence nonnegative given alpha_j >= 0","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E02","v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E11"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::x::q_imp"},{"variable_id":"q_exp","symbol":"q_exp","meaning":"Total energy exported by the community to the system operator","kind":"operational","domain":"continuous; q_exp = sum_{j=1}^n beta_j (1e), hence nonnegative given beta_j >= 0","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E02","v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E11"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::x::q_exp"},{"variable_id":"u_ij","symbol":"u_ij","meaning":"Power set-point of asset i of prosumer j (positive for energy produced)","kind":"operational","domain":"U_ij (feasible space of u_ij); negative values used for energy consumption in the case study","unit":null,"indices":["j = 1, ..., n","i in I_j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E02","v141-b-g01-E12","v141-b-g01-E15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::x::u_ij"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"theta","symbol":"theta","meaning":"Set of relevant parameters or additional variables of the community-manager model g","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"set of relevant parameters or additional variables"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E11"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::theta::theta"},{"parameter_id":"n","symbol":"n","meaning":"Number of prosumers in the collective","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"positive integer (number of prosumers)"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E02","v141-b-g01-E09"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::theta::n"}]},{"unit_id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-a-M02::clearing.local","paper_id":"26-battery_flexibility","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M02","function":"clearing.local","source_occurrences":[{"id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R008","market_meaning":"Charges for discharging the central battery and compensation for charging it enter the local-allocation objective as additional monetary components. Scope: Pool Hub local-allocation objective with centrally owned community storage. The allocation objective minimises grid-consumption costs plus central-battery discharge costs minus charging compensation. Internal P2P trade costs do not affect the community objective value. Scope: Pool Hub community allocation optimisation with centrally owned storage. Allocations obey the central battery's physical limits and a shared storage balance that aggregates charging and discharging flows across all houses. Scope: Pool Hub allocation feasibility with one shared central battery.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Pool Hub allocation feasibility with one shared central battery. | Pool Hub community allocation optimisation with centrally owned storage. | Pool Hub local-allocation objective with centrally owned community storage.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e14","v141-b-g02-e15","v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g03-E11"]},"constraint_refs":["26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C001","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C002","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C003","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C004"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"6f03d5dc7c2a468808ca21954b5e7e46d7d4fa74e880a279cf66d8f68dff37b4"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R008-C001","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Charges for discharging the central battery and compensation for charging it enter the local-allocation objective as additional monetary components. Scope: Pool Hub local-allocation objective with centrally owned community storage.","expression":"\\min \\sum_{h} \\left( \\sum_{t} \\left[p_G^{(t)} \\cdot G^{(t,h)}\\right] + \\sum_{t} \\left[p_D^{(t)} \\cdot D^{(t,h)}\\right] - \\sum_{t} \\left[p_C^{(t)} \\cdot C^{(t,h)}\\right] \\right)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["x_G","x_D","x_C"],"parameter_refs":["theta_p_G","theta_p_D","theta_p_C"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e14","v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g02-e17"],"ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p06-c1r1__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.2 Market specific constraints","anchor_text":"A. Lüth et al. Grid consumption Battery discharge Battery charge re ⎛    ⎞ min ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In the Pool Hub case with a centralised storage, costs arise for three components: grid consumption, P2P trade and discharging the centralised battery. Furthermore, we compensate prosumers for charging and considers their incomes from P2P trade, see Fig. 2(b). The objective function of the Pool Hub, Eq. (11), needs hence two more components that add costs for battery discharging D^{(t,h)} and compensations for charging C^{(t,h)}. \\min \\sum_h \\left( \\sum_t [p_G^{(t)} \\cdot G^{(t,h)}] + \\sum_t [p_D^{(t)} \\cdot D^{(t,h)}] - \\sum_t [p_C^{(t)} \\cdot C^{(t,h)}] \\right) (11)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.3 Pool Hub market results","anchor_text":"ho Half of the demand can be covered from distributed generation • an within the community. sy ra 4.","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"The Pool Hub market design considers an interconnected community of consumers with a central storage device. P2P trade is priced at the same dynamic rate as in the Flexi User case. As the storage is no longer owned privately, discharging from the central battery will be priced and charging compensated. Charging the battery from the grid is not allowed."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p07-c1r4__v141-a-M02-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1.4 Electricity prices","anchor_text":"step. Since this P2P price should not consider the fees for network and sp grid usage (typically one","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For the Pool Hub market, we need two more dynamic price schemes. As the charging of the central battery should be compensated, prosumers receive the wholesale spot price for electricity when they charge the battery. The discharging is priced according to each house’s willingness to pay (i.e. the P2P price p_{p2p}^{(t,h)}) plus an additional fee to avoid arbitrage operation through the battery. This additional fee is set equal to the charging compensation p_C^{(t)} at this time. Thus, the price for discharging is equal to the sum of the P2P trade price p_{p2p}^{(t,h)} and the charging compensation p_C^{(t)} and differs among the houses."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R008-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The allocation objective minimises grid-consumption costs plus central-battery discharge costs minus charging compensation. Internal P2P trade costs do not affect the community objective value. Scope: Pool Hub community allocation optimisation with centrally owned storage.","expression":"\\min \\sum_{h} \\left( \\sum_{t} \\left[p_G^{(t)} \\cdot G^{(t,h)}\\right] + \\sum_{t} \\left[p_D^{(t)} \\cdot D^{(t,h)}\\right] - \\sum_{t} \\left[p_C^{(t)} \\cdot C^{(t,h)}\\right] \\right)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["x_G","x_D","x_C"],"parameter_refs":["theta_p_G","theta_p_D","theta_p_C"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e14","v141-b-g03-E11","v141-b-g02-e18"],"ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p06-c1r1__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.2 Market specific constraints","anchor_text":"A. Lüth et al. Grid consumption Battery discharge Battery charge re ⎛    ⎞ min ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In the Pool Hub case with a centralised storage, costs arise for three components: grid consumption, P2P trade and discharging the centralised battery. Furthermore, we compensate prosumers for charging and considers their incomes from P2P trade, see Fig. 2(b). The objective function of the Pool Hub, Eq. (11), needs hence two more components that add costs for battery discharging D^{(t,h)} and compensations for charging C^{(t,h)}. \\min \\sum_h \\left( \\sum_t [p_G^{(t)} \\cdot G^{(t,h)}] + \\sum_t [p_D^{(t)} \\cdot D^{(t,h)}] - \\sum_t [p_C^{(t)} \\cdot C^{(t,h)}] \\right) (11)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p09-c1r3__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.4 Comparison","anchor_text":"Fig. 4. Demand cover for exemplary houses on changed the generation and demand proﬁles of the houses","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"As trade costs do not affect the objective value of the community, the match of aggregated supply and demand influences the results."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.3 Pool Hub market results","anchor_text":"ho Half of the demand can be covered from distributed generation • an within the community. sy ra 4.","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"The Pool Hub market design considers an interconnected community of consumers with a central storage device. P2P trade is priced at the same dynamic rate as in the Flexi User case. As the storage is no longer owned privately, discharging from the central battery will be priced and charging compensated. Charging the battery from the grid is not allowed."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R008-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Allocations obey the central battery's physical limits and a shared storage balance that aggregates charging and discharging flows across all houses. Scope: Pool Hub allocation feasibility with one shared central battery.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["x_S","x_C","x_D"],"parameter_refs":["theta_s_lower","theta_s_upper","theta_alpha","theta_beta","theta_eta_c","theta_eta_d"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e15"],"ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e15","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p06-c1r1__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.2 Market specific constraints","anchor_text":"A. Lüth et al. Grid consumption Battery discharge Battery charge re ⎛    ⎞ min ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The big battery is limited to the physical constraints formulated in Eqs. (7)–(9) with the constants \\underline{s}, \\bar{s}, \\alpha, \\beta, \\eta^c and \\eta^d being different for the greater centralised storage entity. The overall storage level will no longer be depending on a single house’s charge or discharge, but takes into account the sum of all flows from and to all houses to the battery. Eq. (12) comprises the sums of discharge D^{(t,h)} and charge C^{(t,h)} for the centralised battery and adds these up to the overall storage level S^{(t)}. S^{(t)} = S^{(t-1)} + \\eta^c \\cdot \\sum_h C^{(t,h)} - (1/\\eta^d) \\cdot \\sum_h D^{(t,h)}, \\quad \\forall t \\in T. (12)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R008-C004","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The shared central-battery storage balance updates the aggregate storage level from the previous time step using efficiency-weighted sums of all houses' charging and discharging flows.","expression":"S^{(t)} = S^{(t-1)} + \\eta^c \\cdot \\sum_{h} C^{(t,h)} - (1/\\eta^d) \\cdot \\sum_{h} D^{(t,h)}, \\quad \\forall t \\in T","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["x_S","x_C","x_D"],"parameter_refs":["theta_eta_c","theta_eta_d"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e15"],"ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e15","location":{"location_id":"src-9788b5496d-p06-c1r1__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.2 Market specific constraints","anchor_text":"A. Lüth et al. Grid consumption Battery discharge Battery charge re ⎛    ⎞ min ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The big battery is limited to the physical constraints formulated in Eqs. (7)–(9) with the constants \\underline{s}, \\bar{s}, \\alpha, \\beta, \\eta^c and \\eta^d being different for the greater centralised storage entity. The overall storage level will no longer be depending on a single house’s charge or discharge, but takes into account the sum of all flows from and to all houses to the battery. Eq. (12) comprises the sums of discharge D^{(t,h)} and charge C^{(t,h)} for the centralised battery and adds these up to the overall storage level S^{(t)}. S^{(t)} = S^{(t-1)} + \\eta^c \\cdot \\sum_h C^{(t,h)} - (1/\\eta^d) \\cdot \\sum_h D^{(t,h)}, \\quad \\forall t \\in T. (12)."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"x_G","symbol":"G^{(t,h)}","meaning":"Grid consumption of house h in time step t in the Pool Hub clearing problem; a model decision variable entering the grid-cost term of Eq. (11).","kind":"operational","domain":"Grid-consumption quantity for each house h and time step t; the supplied evidence does not state an explicit numeric domain or separate nonnegativity condition.","unit":null,"indices":["t","h"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e14"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::x::x_G"},{"variable_id":"x_D","symbol":"D^{(t,h)}","meaning":"Discharge from the centralised battery attributed to house h in time step t; the source labels D as discharge of the battery and Eq. (12) sums D over houses for the central battery.","kind":"operational","domain":"Discharge flow for each house h and time step t; rate bounds are referred to Eqs. (7)-(9), but the exact central-battery printed rate-limit expression is not present in the supplied evidence.","unit":null,"indices":["t","h"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e14","v141-b-g02-e15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::x::x_D"},{"variable_id":"x_C","symbol":"C^{(t,h)}","meaning":"Charge flow into the centralised battery attributed to house h in time step t; the source labels C as charge of the battery and Eq. (12) sums C over houses for the central battery.","kind":"operational","domain":"Charge flow for each house h and time step t; rate bounds are referred to Eqs. (7)-(9), but the exact central-battery printed rate-limit expression is not present in the supplied evidence.","unit":null,"indices":["t","h"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e14","v141-b-g02-e15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::x::x_C"},{"variable_id":"x_S","symbol":"S^{(t)}","meaning":"Overall storage level of the centralised community battery in time step t, the aggregate state variable in Eq. (12).","kind":"operational","domain":"Storage level of the centralised battery at time step t; bounded by the physical limits referenced from Eqs. (7)-(9), whose central-battery form is not printed in the supplied evidence.","unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::x::x_S"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"theta_p_G","symbol":"p_G^{(t)}","meaning":"Price of electricity from the grid in time step t; the exogenous grid-price parameter multiplying G^{(t,h)} in Eq. (11).","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e14"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::theta::theta_p_G"},{"parameter_id":"theta_p_D","symbol":"p_D^{(t)}","meaning":"Central-battery discharge price in time step t as printed in Eq. (11). Other supplied passages use a house-indexed p_D^{(t,h)} and describe the discharge price as p_{p2p}^{(t,h)} + p_C^{(t)}; the exact indexing is unresolved in the supplied excerpts.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e14","v141-b-g02-e17"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::theta::theta_p_D"},{"parameter_id":"theta_p_C","symbol":"p_C^{(t)}","meaning":"Compensation rate for charging the central battery in time step t; Eq. (11) subtracts p_C^{(t)} C^{(t,h)} from the objective. The text states charging is compensated at the wholesale spot price, but no explicit equality is printed in the supplied excerpts.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e14","v141-b-g02-e17"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::theta::theta_p_C"},{"parameter_id":"theta_eta_c","symbol":"\\eta^c","meaning":"Charging efficiency coefficient for the centralised battery in Eq. (12); the source states the constants differ from the private battery, but the central-battery numeric value is not given in the supplied evidence.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::theta::theta_eta_c"},{"parameter_id":"theta_eta_d","symbol":"\\eta^d","meaning":"Discharging efficiency coefficient for the centralised battery in Eq. (12), entering as 1/\\eta^d; the central-battery numeric value is not given in the supplied evidence.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::theta::theta_eta_d"},{"parameter_id":"theta_s_lower","symbol":"\\underline{s}","meaning":"Lower bound on the central battery storage level, one of the physical constants referred to via Eqs. (7)-(9) as applied to the centralised battery; the central-battery value is not printed in the supplied excerpts.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::theta::theta_s_lower"},{"parameter_id":"theta_s_upper","symbol":"\\bar{s}","meaning":"Upper bound on the central battery storage level, one of the physical constants referred to via Eqs. (7)-(9) as applied to the centralised battery; the central-battery value is not printed in the supplied excerpts.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::theta::theta_s_upper"},{"parameter_id":"theta_alpha","symbol":"\\alpha","meaning":"Maximum charging rate constant for the central battery, referred to via Eqs. (7)-(9) with a different value for the centralised battery; the exact central-battery value is not printed in the supplied excerpts.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::theta::theta_alpha"},{"parameter_id":"theta_beta","symbol":"\\beta","meaning":"Maximum discharging rate constant for the central battery, referred to via Eqs. (7)-(9) with a different value for the centralised battery; the exact central-battery value is not printed in the supplied excerpts.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R008","declaration_ref":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::theta::theta_beta"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"04-energy_collectives::v141-a-M01::clearing.social_welfare","stage":"L5","subtype":"aggregate_cost_minimization","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Collective configuration minimizes Σ_j f̃_j(p_j,q_j,α_j,β_j)+g(q_imp,q_exp,θ), where f̃_j=Σ_{i∈I_j}ψ_ij(u_ij)+h_j(q_j,α_j,β_j)+I_{R_j}(p_j,q_j,α_j,β_j).","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: the overall objective of collective energy dispatch","Core operation: minimize after aggregating asset cost or utility terms, member preferences or transaction costs, and the manager objective","Output: the optimal configuration under this objective"],"variation_fields":["Specific forms of ψ_ij, h_j, R_j, and the manager model g","The global optimality guarantee is limited to cases that preserve overall convexity","The equilibrium formulation assumes perfect competition","The centralized formulation requires members to disclose utility curves"],"constraint_refs":["04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C004","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C005"],"paper_id":"04-energy_collectives","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"clearing.social_welfare","template_code":"JOC"},{"unit_id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-a-M02::clearing.local","stage":"L5","subtype":"community_net_energy_cost_minimizing_allocation","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Jointly configure community electricity purchase and public battery charging/discharging, minimizing ∑_h(∑_t p_G^{(t)}G^{(t,h)}+∑_t p_D^{(t)}D^{(t,h)}−∑_t p_C^{(t)}C^{(t,h)}); subject to the physical limits of the public battery and S^{(t)}=S^{(t-1)}+η^c∑_h C^{(t,h)}−(1/η^d)∑_h D^{(t,h)} constraint.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: community grid electricity purchase, each household's public battery charging/discharging shares, and the shared energy storage state","Core operation: within the feasible region of shared energy storage, minimize electricity purchase and discharge costs minus charging compensation","Output: each household's electricity purchase and charging/discharging configuration in each time period, and the public battery state"],"variation_fields":["Internal P2P revenues and expenditures do not affect the community objective value","Public battery capacity, rate, and efficiency parameters","The specific rate-limit expression for the central battery is not expanded in the attached evidence","It is not permitted to use grid electricity to charge the public battery","The original objective uses p_D^{(t)}, while other quotations use p_D^{(t,h)}; the indexing difference is retained"],"constraint_refs":["26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C001","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C002","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C003","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C004"],"paper_id":"26-battery_flexibility","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M02","function":"clearing.local","template_code":"JOC"}],"source_variants":[{"unit_id":"04-energy_collectives::v141-a-M01::clearing.social_welfare","source_definition":"Collective configuration minimizes Σ_j f̃_j(p_j,q_j,α_j,β_j)+g(q_imp,q_exp,θ), where f̃_j=Σ_{i∈I_j}ψ_ij(u_ij)+h_j(q_j,α_j,β_j)+I_{R_j}(p_j,q_j,α_j,β_j).","retained_variants":["Retain ψ_ij, h_j, and the independent manager function g; the common agreement can change through g and cannot be deleted into a mere electricity purchase expenditure.","Retain the roles and signs of net asset production, internal exchange, external imports, and exports; q_j is real while α_j and β_j are nonnegative.","Retain member balance, internal exchange balance, and the coupling of community total imports and total exports.","The global optimality guarantee is limited to cases that preserve overall convexity; nonconvex cases do not obtain this guarantee.","The centralized formulation requires disclosure of utility curves, and the equilibrium formulation assumes perfect competition; the KKT and efficiency statements are property descriptions and do not change the identity of the general aggregate objective here."],"constraint_refs":["04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C004","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C005","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C006"]},{"unit_id":"26-battery_flexibility::v141-a-M02::clearing.local","source_definition":"Jointly configure community electricity purchase and public battery charging/discharging, minimizing ∑_h(∑_t p_G^{(t)}G^{(t,h)}+∑_t p_D^{(t)}D^{(t,h)}−∑_t p_C^{(t)}C^{(t,h)}); subject to the physical limits of the public battery and S^{(t)}=S^{(t-1)}+η^c∑_h C^{(t,h)}−(1/η^d)∑_h D^{(t,h)} constraint.","retained_variants":["Electricity purchase and discharge charges are positive expenditures, and charging compensation enters member net cost with a negative sign; compensation cannot be rewritten as physical depreciation cost.","Internal P2P revenues and expenditures do not affect the community objective value; public battery charges and compensation are still retained.","The shared energy storage state is updated according to all households' flows and charging/discharging efficiencies and is not split into independent private batteries.","Using grid electricity to charge the public battery is prohibited; capacity and rate conditions refer to equations (7) to (9), and parts not expanded are not supplemented.","The original objective uses p_D^{(t)}, while the price description uses household-specific p_D^{(t,h)} and associates it with their P2P prices; the two notations are stored separately, and no uniform discharge rate is presumed.","The textual description that charging is compensated at the wholesale spot price is retained; no price equation or numerical domain that is not provided is added."],"constraint_refs":["26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C001","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C002","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C003","26-battery_flexibility::v141-c-R008::v141-c-R008-C004"]}],"source_parent_definition":{"definition":"Directly aggregate the economic contributions of collective members' asset operation and energy exchange, retain additional economic terms according to the source, and minimize this composite objective within the resource-coupled feasible region to select the joint energy configuration.","object":"Energy exchange and asset operation of collective members and their aggregate economic evaluation; household-level shares and public states of shared energy storage remain distinct.","inputs":"Member cost or utility functions, preferences and trading rules, equipment charging and compensation parameters, as well as the manager objective and resource feasibility conditions applicable to the source.","operation":"Sum member contributions and applicable additional terms according to each economic attribution and revenue/expenditure sign, and jointly minimize the objective; independent manager functions and member equipment revenues/expenditures are not forcibly mapped to the same term.","outputs":"The aggregate economic objective and corresponding member configurations; the public battery member additionally retains a shared state trajectory.","boundary":"Limited to L5/JOC; retain the member economic term aggregation identity of KG31-e0d9a80700a2295a, without presupposing a unified welfare net surplus meaning. Exclude taking welfare net surplus as a defining criterion, purchasing fixed demand solely according to supplier bids, minimizing the highest individual cost, and purely feasibility-based exchange. Charging, compensation direction, and price-sharing methods must not be unified."},"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/151"});
